explanation:
This CSS goes in the stylesheet for the previous fragment.

```css
.toolbar {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 14px;
  justify-content: flex-start;
}
@media (max-width: 460px) {
  .toolbar {
    gap: 7px;
  }
}
```

display selects Flexbox. flex-wrap permits extra lines. The @media block applies its inner rule only when the viewport is at most 460px wide. A viewport is the browser area used to display the page. At narrower widths the gap is smaller. This query changes spacing; wrapping is still enabled in both cases.

This fragment styles a gallery whose direct children are images. Put it in the gallery page's CSS editor and supply the HTML from the later example.

```css
.gallery {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
  gap: 12px;
}
.gallery img {
  display: block;
  width: 100%;
  height: auto;
}
@media (max-width: 520px) {
  .gallery {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
  }
}
```

At wider viewport widths, this example uses four equal columns. At 520px and below it uses two. The exercise uses a different wide column count and threshold. width: 100% makes each image fill its own cell, while height: auto keeps its proportions.

