explanation:
This CSS fragment styles a bar element. The exercise requires a particular animation name; this separate example uses grow.

```css
.bar {
  width: 100%;
  height: 16px;
  background: #245e95;
  animation: grow 3s linear both;
}
@keyframes grow {
  from {
    width: 0;
  }
  to {
    width: 100%;
  }
}
```

@keyframes names the sequence. from is its start and to is its end. width: 100% uses the containing block's width. The animation shorthand supplies a name, duration, timing function, and fill mode. 3s means three seconds. linear means progress through the animated change advances evenly with time.

Put this HTML fragment in body for the bar, and append the CSS query in the same stylesheet as the earlier rules.

```html
<p class="progress">Download complete: 100%</p>
<div class="bar" role="img" aria-label="100 percent complete"></div>
```

```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .bar {
    animation: none;
    width: 100%;
  }
}
```

The paragraph keeps the result visible. role="img" gives this decorative-shaped representation an image role, and aria-label supplies its text meaning. The query removes motion and preserves the final width. This example is a completion illustration, not a full live progressbar implementation.

