explanation:
This standalone example passes a function into another.
```javascript
function wave(name) {
  return `Hi, ${name}!`;
}
function welcome(makeMessage, name) {
  return makeMessage(name);
}
const greeting = welcome(wave, "Noor");
console.log(greeting);
```
It prints `Hi, Noor!`. The first argument fills makeMessage with the wave function. Inside welcome, `makeMessage(name)` calls wave with Noor. The returned string travels back through welcome to greeting.

Two callbacks let the same receiving function choose two styles.
```javascript
function wave(name) {
  return `Hi, ${name}!`;
}
function cheer(name) {
  return `Great to see you, ${name}!`;
}
function welcome(makeMessage, name) {
  return makeMessage(name);
}
console.log(welcome(wave, "Noor"));
console.log(welcome(cheer, "Noor"));
```
The outputs are `Hi, Noor!` and `Great to see you, Noor!`. Welcome has not changed; only the passed function differs. Returning text lets either message be displayed or used elsewhere.

