  // TODO: filter >=30, convert to seconds, total and average
  return { total: 0, average: null };
}
const result = summarize([20, 35, 45]);
console.log(result.total, result.average);

solution:
function summarize(minutes) { const seconds = minutes.filter(m => m >= 30).map(m => m * 60); const total = seconds.reduce((sum, x) => sum + x, 0); return { total, average: seconds.length ? total / seconds.length : null }; }
const result = summarize([20, 35, 45]);
console.log(result.total, result.average);

explanation:
Here is the complete browser script for that markup.
```javascript
function updatePreview() {
  const name = document.querySelector("#name").value;
  const color = document.querySelector("#color").value;
  const preview = document.querySelector("#preview");
  preview.textContent = name;
  preview.style.color = color;
}
document.querySelector("#name").addEventListener("input", updatePreview);
document.querySelector("#color").addEventListener("input", updatePreview);
updatePreview();
```
The function reads both current values and writes them to the preview. The final call initially displays Noor in purple. Without that call, the listener setup alone would not fill the initial preview.

Prefer plain text for words users enter. If someone types `<b>Noor</b>`, textContent displays those characters rather than turning them into a bold element. `.innerHTML` interprets text as HTML, which can cause unintended behavior with user input. The same safe display principle applies to any typed name. Setting the color uses a separate style property and does not require changing the HTML.

For a second worked example, use the same HTML markup in a fresh page and run this complete browser script after it:
```javascript
function updatePreview() {
  const name = document.querySelector("#name").value;
  const color = document.querySelector("#color").value;
  const preview = document.querySelector("#preview");
  preview.textContent = name;
  preview.style.color = color;
}
document.querySelector("#name").value = "<b>Echo</b>";
document.querySelector("#color").value = "blue";
updatePreview();
console.log(document.querySelector("#preview").textContent);
console.log(document.querySelector("#preview").style.color);
```
The output lines are literally `<b>Echo</b>` and blue. The paragraph also shows the angle brackets as text in blue, rather than creating a bold element. These assignments simulate changed input values; calling updatePreview then reads the changed values. The first example's registered listeners do this reading automatically after a real input event.

