This is a complete top-level asynchronous Swift program for a compatible modern Swift toolchain. It demonstrates waiting and an error path without a user interface.

```swift
enum SampleError: Error {
    case unavailable
}

func loadNames(_ request: Int) async throws -> [String] {
    try await Task.sleep(for: .milliseconds(20))
    try Task.checkCancellation()
    if request == 0 {
        throw SampleError.unavailable
    }
    return ["name-\(request)"]
}

do {
    let names = try await loadNames(7)
    print(names)
} catch is CancellationError {
    print("Cancelled")
} catch {
    print("Could not load names")
}
```

The ordinary successful result is ["name-7"]. throws says the function can fail by throwing an Error. try marks calls that may throw, and do/catch handles the result. Cancellation has its own catch so it can be described differently from a failed load. enum defines a type with named cases; here unavailable is one possible error value. The colon in : Error declares that the type meets Swift's error requirements. A function parameter names an input, while -> [String] promises an array of text as its successful result.

This separate complete top-level async program demonstrates actor storage rejecting an older replacement. It can run with a compatible Swift toolchain; it has no SwiftUI screen.

```swift
func accepts(request: Int, current: Int) -> Bool {
    request == current
}

actor MessageStore {
    private var values: [String] = []
    private var requestID = 0

    func replace(with newValues: [String], request: Int) {
        guard request >= requestID else { return }
        requestID = request
        values = newValues
    }

    func snapshot() -> [String] {
        values
    }
}

let store = MessageStore()
await store.replace(with: ["new result"], request: 9)
await store.replace(with: ["old result"], request: 8)
let saved = await store.snapshot()
print(saved)
print(accepts(request: 8, current: 9))
```

The output is ["new result"] and false. The first call establishes request 9. The request-8 guard exits without changing values. snapshot returns the isolated array through the actor's method. This small example tests the ordering rule; it does not simulate concurrent completion by itself.

