explanation:
This complete TypeScript setup models a smaller delivery workflow:
```typescript
type DeliveryState =
  | { kind: 'idle' }
  | { kind: 'loading'; requestId: number }
  | { kind: 'success'; requestId: number; data: string[] }
  | { kind: 'error'; requestId: number; message: string }
  | { kind: 'cancelled'; requestId: number };
type DeliveryAction =
  | { type: 'start'; requestId: number }
  | { type: 'resolve'; requestId: number; data: string[] }
  | { type: 'reject'; requestId: number; message: string }
  | { type: 'cancel'; requestId: number };
const waiting: DeliveryState = { kind: 'idle' };
console.log(waiting.kind);
```
It prints idle. string[] is an array of text. The union bars mean one valid shape at a time; the state is not simultaneously success and error.

Continue after the type declarations:
```typescript
function nextState(state: DeliveryState, action: DeliveryAction): DeliveryState {
  if (action.type === 'start') {
    return { kind: 'loading', requestId: action.requestId };
  }
  if (state.kind !== 'loading' || state.requestId !== action.requestId) {
    return state;
  }
  if (action.type === 'resolve') {
    return { kind: 'success', requestId: action.requestId, data: action.data };
  }
  if (action.type === 'reject') {
    return { kind: 'error', requestId: action.requestId, message: action.message };
  }
  return { kind: 'cancelled', requestId: action.requestId };
}
const active = nextState(waiting, { type: 'start', requestId: 8 });
const old = nextState(active, { type: 'resolve', requestId: 7, data: ['old'] });
console.log(active.kind);
console.log(old === active);
```
It prints loading and true. The old response returns the identical state object rather than constructing a misleading new success.

A third example in the same file shows cancellation:
```typescript
const stopped = nextState(active, { type: 'cancel', requestId: 8 });
const late = nextState(stopped, {
  type: 'resolve', requestId: 8, data: ['late parcel']
});
const failed = nextState(active, {
  type: 'reject', requestId: 8, message: 'Offline'
});
console.log(stopped.kind);
console.log(late === stopped);
console.log(failed.kind);
```
The outputs are cancelled, true, error. Cancellation here prevents a late result changing the model; it does not actually stop a network request or timer. Real cancellation can also stop work with an appropriate API.

