explanation:
This standalone JavaScript example models a route decision without a real network:
```javascript
const visits = [];
function handle(method, path, body = {}) {
  if (method === 'GET' && path === '/visits') {
    return { status: 200, body: [...visits] };
  }
  if (method === 'POST' && path === '/visits') {
    if (!Number.isInteger(body.minutes) || body.minutes < 1 || body.minutes > 180) {
      return { status: 400, body: { error: 'Use 1 to 180 whole minutes' } };
    }
    const visit = { id: visits.length + 1, minutes: body.minutes };
    visits.push(visit);
    return { status: 201, body: visit };
  }
  return { status: 404, body: { error: 'Not found' } };
}
console.log(handle('POST', '/visits', { minutes: 45 }));
console.log(handle('GET', '/visits'));
```
The first response is status 201 with {id: 1, minutes: 45}; the second is 200 with an array containing that saved visit.

Express is a package for writing HTTP routes in Node.js, which runs JavaScript outside the browser. Save this complete separate example as server.mjs in a local project with the supplied Express dependency installed:
```javascript
import express from 'express';
const app = express();
app.use(express.json({ limit: '20kb' }));
const visits = [];
app.get('/visits', (req, res) => res.json(visits));
app.post('/visits', (req, res) => {
  const minutes = req.body?.minutes;
  if (!Number.isInteger(minutes) || minutes < 1 || minutes > 180) {
    return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Use 1 to 180 whole minutes' });
  }
  const visit = { id: crypto.randomUUID(), minutes };
  visits.push(visit);
  res.status(201).json(visit);
});
app.listen(3000, '127.0.0.1', () => console.log('Local server ready'));
```
Run with Node.js 22.13 or newer. The callback receives req for request information and res for the response. listen starts the local server on port 3000.

With that server running, this JavaScript fragment can be used in a browser console on the same local origin, or in a Node environment supporting fetch:
```javascript
const created = await fetch('http://127.0.0.1:3000/visits', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
  body: JSON.stringify({ minutes: 45 })
});
console.log(created.status);
const response = await fetch('http://127.0.0.1:3000/visits');
console.log(await response.json());
```
It prints 201 then the saved array. JSON.stringify turns the object into request text. await waits for each promise's result; response.json parses returned JSON. Cross-origin browser pages may need CORS permission, so prefer the local server's origin or Node for this walkthrough.

